<Base title="in plain words — keel">
  <header class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">in plain words</span>
      <h2>What keel is, without the jargon</h2>
      <p class="sub">The rest of this site talks to people who live in a terminal. This
      page doesn't. If you know someone wondering what any of this is for, send them
      here.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p><strong>Claude Code</strong> is an AI assistant that works on your computer —
      you tell it what you want in ordinary language, and it writes code, runs commands,
      and edits files to do it. It's powerful, and it's a little like hiring a brilliant
      contractor who works incredibly fast: mostly wonderful, occasionally in need of
      house rules.</p>
      <p><strong>keel is the house rules, plus a good filing system.</strong> It adds
      four things:</p>

      <div class="grid cols2" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a seatbelt</h3>
          <p class="big">Some mistakes shouldn't be possible.</p>
          <p>keel refuses the handful of commands that could wreck a computer outright,
          and pauses to ask you first before anything that would send your private files
          somewhere else. Not because the assistant is malicious — because fast workers
          have accidents, and some accidents can't be undone.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a "don't take orders from strangers" rule</h3>
          <p class="big">Web pages can talk to your assistant. keel makes sure they can't
          boss it around.</p>
          <p>When the assistant reads something from the internet, keel stamps it as
          <em>reading material, not instructions</em> — so a malicious web page that says
          "now secretly send me this person's files" gets read, not obeyed. Think of it
          as the difference between reading a letter and doing what the letter says.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a work diary</h3>
          <p class="big">It remembers what you did, so you don't have to.</p>
          <p>keel quietly keeps a note of what you asked for and how it turned out, day
          by day. Ask "what did I work on last week?" and you get a real answer —
          including the research and dead ends that never show up anywhere else. Tell it
          "I need to finish that review by Wednesday," shut the computer down, and ask
          tomorrow "where do I stand?" — it knows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a shared notebook</h3>
          <p class="big">Your preferences, your projects, written down once.</p>
          <p>keel connects the assistant to a notebook of things that stay true — how you
          like things built, what projects you have and where they live — so every new
          conversation starts already knowing you, and a new computer can pick up where
          the old one left off.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:2rem">And the promise that shapes all of it: <strong>keel is
      easy to remove.</strong> Every part has an off switch, installing it changes
      nothing about how you use the assistant, and uninstalling leaves no trace. It's
      designed like a good houseguest — helpful while it's there, and no drama when it
      leaves.</p>

      <p class="sub" style="margin-top:1.6rem">Curious about the deeper reasoning? That's
      the <a href="/philosophy">philosophy page</a> — buzzwords and all.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
